{
  "B_expression": "-1/(2*(1+u)*(sqrt((1+u)**2 + r) + (1+u))**2)",
  "C_expression": "-1/(2*(1+u))",
  "derivation": "Revised from the retained source-compatible pair to its exact positive-root continuation. Let s=1+u and A=sqrt(s*s+r). Since z=1+u and r=p-z^2, the positive square-root increment to the target is g*=A-s=r/(A+s). Subtracting the linear reference r/2 gives g*-r/2 = r/(A+s)-r/2. Using r=(A-s)(A+s) and s=1+u, this decomposes exactly as r^2*(-1/(2*s*(A+s)**2)) + u*r*(-1/(2*s)). Therefore B(r,u)=-1/(2*(1+u)*(sqrt((1+u)**2+r)+(1+u))**2) and C(r,u)=-1/(2*(1+u)) give g = r/2 + chi_r(r)*chi_u(u)*(r^2*B + u*r*C) = r/2 + chi*(g*-r/2). In the full support where chi_r=chi_u=1, g=g* and z_after=sqrt(p), so the whole signed residual E=(z+g)^2-p vanishes up to floating-point roundoff. At u=0, B reduces to the source-only canceler -1/(2*(1+sqrt(1+r))**2) and C=-1/2, agreeing with the previous program on its core. Both bodies are analytic wherever 1+u>0 and (1+u)^2+r>0. On the closed outer rectangle, 1+u>=3/4 and (1+u)^2+r>=1/16, so there is positive margin; for example the open neighborhood -0.26<u<0.26 and -0.51<r<0.51 is analytic. This is a source-relative coefficient-family construction only, not a full NS proof or full PDE certificate. Outside the chi support the host uses only the linear reference g=r/2.",
  "inspect": [],
  "note": "Successor should check that APPLIED rows in the full chi support (|r|<=1/4 and |u|<=1/8) now show abs_residual at roundoff scale rather than the previous O(r*u^2) scale. Rows in the chi transition zones (1/8<|u|<1/4 or 1/4<|r|<1/2) receive partial correction and can have larger but smooth residuals. Rows with |u|>=1/4 or |r|>=1/2 correctly receive only g=r/2 and may retain large residuals, especially far LEFT/RIGHT rows. The state variable u is not reset; each row uses its stored z to form u=z-1 and r=p-z^2. The analytic certificate is sufficient for this coefficient family on a core neighborhood; it is not a participation criterion and does not establish all-order smoothness or a full NS/PDE certificate."
}